m:**\n- **State Machine:** Use a state machine to represent the current state of the system.\n- **Graph Representation:** Represent the world as a graph for better understanding and manipulation.\n\n**Limitations/Tradeoffs:**\n- **Complexity:** State machines and graphs can become complex and difficult to manage.\n- **Performance:** Graph representations can be computationally expensive.\n\n### 6. Safety and Governance\n\n**Concrete Mechanism:**\n- **Verifier:** Implement a verifier to ensure that tool actions are safe and do not violate user permissions.\n- **Governance Policies:** Define governance policies to control the execution of tool actions.\n\n**Limitations/Tradeoffs:**\n- **Verification Overhead:** Verification can introduce overhead and delay in action execution.\n- **Policy Enforcement:** Enforcing governance policies can be challenging and may require additional resources.\n\n### 7. Evaluation and Benchmarks\n\n**Concrete Mechanism:**\n- **Performance Benchmarks:** Measure the performance of the system using benchmarks.\n- **User Feedback:** Collect user feedback to evaluate the system's usability and effectiveness.\n\n**Limitations/Tradeoffs:**\n- **Benchmark Accuracy:** Benchmarks may not accurately reflect real-world performance.\n- **User Subjectivity:** User feedback can be subjective and may not be representative of all users.\n\n### 8. Persistence and Runtime Recovery\n\n**Concrete Mechanism:**\n- **File Patches:** Implement file patches to handle file edits and recover from crashes.\n- **Persistent State:** Use a persistent state system to maintain the system's state across restarts.\n\n**Limitations/Tradeoffs:**\n- **File Patches:** File patches can be complex and error-prone.\n- **Persistent State:** Maintaining a persistent state can be resource-intensive.\n\n### 9. Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\n**Concrete Mechanism:**\n- **Agent Coordination:** Implement a coordination mechanism to manage multiple agents.\n- **Communication Protocols:** Use 